# short-description: Pi Zero W serial logger: boot, rootfs, and a captures partition
# long-description: meta-raspberrypi's sdimage-raspberrypi.wks plus a third
# partition that holds the logger's captures and grows to fill the card on
# first boot.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS FILE IS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# wic (poky/scripts/wic) is the OpenEmbedded Image Creator: "oeic", with the
# hard-to-say "oe" swapped for a "w", per poky's own dev-manual/wic.rst. It
# is the build step
# that turns the build's PIECES (the root filesystem directory, the kernel,
# device trees, the Pi firmware) into ONE disk image you can write to a
# card. BitBake runs it because meta-raspberrypi's rpi-base.inc lists
# "wic.bz2" in IMAGE_FSTYPES; the result is
# tmp/deploy/images/raspberrypi0-wifi/rpi0-logger-image-*.rootfs.wic.bz2,
# which flash-sd.sh decompresses and writes to the card byte for byte.
# Think of it as a linker script plus objcopy for a flash image.
#
# A .wks ("wic kickstart") file is the layout wic follows: one `part` line
# per partition, in order. For each, wic creates the filesystem as a
# separate file, fills it if there is a --source, then writes the partition
# table (MBR here, see below) and copies each partition into the image at
# its offset. The image recipe picks this file with
# WKS_FILE = "rpi0-logger.wks" (rpi0-logger-image.bb). Before session 28 it
# used meta-raspberrypi's stock sdimage-raspberrypi.wks.
#
# The first two `part` lines are meta-raspberrypi's stock layout, copied
# unchanged. The third is ours.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY A THIRD PARTITION  (session 28)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The logger's own limits stop the LOGGER filling the disk. A separate
# partition contains the damage when something else misbehaves: a full
# captures partition stops logging, a full rootfs stops the board.
#
# Why only 64 MB here, when the plan is "the whole card": the build does not
# know which card the image will land on, and flash-sd.sh writes and then
# reads back every byte of the image. A 14 GB partition built in here would
# mean ~14 GB written and verified on every reflash. So the image carries a
# small partition, and on the first boot grow-captures.service extends it to
# the end of whatever card it is on, then systemd-growfs grows the
# filesystem inside to match (x-systemd.growfs in fstab). See
# recipes-core/captures-partition.
#
# It must be the LAST partition: a partition can only grow into free space
# directly after it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MBR, NOT GPT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The partition table is the small structure at the start of the disk that
# lists where each partition starts and how long it is. Two formats exist:
#
#   MBR  (1983) all of it in sector 0: four 16-byte entries, each holding a
#        type byte, a 32-bit start sector and a 32-bit length. Max 4
#        partitions, max 2 TiB, one copy, no checksum.
#   GPT  (2000s, UEFI PCs) 128 entries, 64-bit sizes, CRC32 checksums, a
#        backup copy at the end of the disk, a unique ID per partition.
#
# This card is MBR because the Zero W's boot ROM and bootcode.bin only read
# MBR (the Pi 4 and 5 boot from GPT too; the Zero W does not). wic uses MBR
# unless a .wks says `bootloader --ptable gpt`, and this one does not.
#
# It is also why systemd-repart, systemd's standard "create or grow
# partitions on first boot" tool, is not used: it finds partitions by their
# GPT type IDs, which MBR does not have.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE `part` LINES, ARGUMENT BY ARGUMENT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   part /boot, part /
#                 The mount point. wic uses it for two things: to write
#                 fstab entries (switched off for this image by
#                 --no-fstab-update in rpi0-logger-image.bb; our own fstab
#                 is authoritative), and, with --source rootfs, to know which
#                 part of the built root filesystem goes in this partition.
#                 The captures line has none: it gets no content from the
#                 build, and fstab mounts it.
#   --source bootimg-partition
#                 A wic plugin that fills the partition with the files listed
#                 in IMAGE_BOOT_FILES: the Pi firmware (bootcode.bin,
#                 start.elf), config.txt, cmdline.txt, the kernel and the
#                 device trees.
#   --source rootfs
#                 A wic plugin that copies in the root filesystem the build
#                 just assembled.
#   (no --source) An empty partition, formatted with --fstype and nothing
#                 else. That is the captures partition.
#   --ondisk mmcblk0
#                 Which disk the partition belongs to. wic can build images
#                 for several disks at once; there is only one here.
#                 mmcblk0 is the Pi's SD card slot.
#   --fstype=vfat / --fstype=ext4
#                 The filesystem to create, which also picks the MBR type
#                 byte: FAT32 for the firmware partition (the boot ROM can
#                 only read FAT), ext4 for the Linux ones.
#   --label boot / root / captures
#                 The filesystem's volume name. Shows up in `lsblk -f` and
#                 /dev/disk/by-label/. Our fstab names partitions by device
#                 path instead; the labels are for humans.
#   --active      Sets the "bootable" flag in this MBR entry. Kept from the
#                 stock layout; the Pi firmware finds the FAT partition
#                 without it.
#   --align 4096  Start the partition on a multiple of 4096 KiB (4 MiB). SD
#                 cards erase and rewrite flash in large internal blocks,
#                 commonly 4 MiB. A partition that starts mid-block makes
#                 many writes straddle two blocks, which costs speed and
#                 wear.
#   --size 100 / --size 64
#                 The partition size, in MiB. The rootfs line has no --size:
#                 wic sizes it from the content times IMAGE_OVERHEAD_FACTOR
#                 (1.3), which is why it is only ~109 MiB.
#   --mkfs-extraopts "..."
#                 Extra options for mkfs.ext4. They REPLACE wic's default for
#                 an empty ext4 partition, which is "-i 8192"
#                 (poky/scripts/lib/wic/partition.py). wic adds -F, -L and
#                 -U itself.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE mkfs.ext4 OPTIONS FOR CAPTURES
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why they matter: the filesystem is CREATED at 64 MB but LIVES at the size
# of the card, and some properties are fixed at creation. Measured 2026-11-07
# with the build's own mke2fs 1.47.0 and its mke2fs.conf, creating at 64 MB
# and growing to this 14.4 GiB card:
#
#                          wic default ("-i 8192")   ours
#     block size           1 KiB                     4 KiB
#     inodes (max files)   1,890,304 (~460 MiB)      29,696 (~7 MiB)
#     reserved for root    5%  (~738 MiB)            0
#
# Both can grow while mounted to 1024x the creation size (see "HOW FAR IT
# CAN GROW" below).
#
#   -b 4096       4 KiB blocks. mke2fs picks 1 KiB for anything under 512 MB
#                 (its "small" profile), and growing never changes the block
#                 size. 1 KiB blocks on a 14 GB partition mean four times as
#                 much bookkeeping for every file.
#   -i 262144     One inode per 256 KiB of the filesystem at creation. Every
#                 file uses one inode, so this is the maximum number of
#                 files. The count per 128 MiB block group is fixed at
#                 creation and every new group gets the same, so it scales
#                 with the card: ~30,000 on 16 GB. Captures are up to 4 MB
#                 each, but a flaky cable makes many tiny ones (seen
#                 2026-11-04), and running out of inodes stops the logger
#                 with the disk still empty. 7 MiB is cheap insurance.
#                 (-T largefile would give 7,424, a limit a bad cable
#                 could actually reach.)
#   -m 0          No root-reserved blocks. ext4 normally keeps 5% that only
#                 root can use, so a system disk filled by users still lets
#                 root log in. Here the only writer IS root, so the reserve
#                 protects nothing, and the logger's LOGGER_KEEP_FREE (10%)
#                 is the headroom that applies.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW FAR IT CAN GROW  (and an option that does NOT work here)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Growing a MOUNTED ext4 needs spare room for its bookkeeping (more block
# group descriptors), set aside when the filesystem is created: "Reserved
# GDT blocks" in dumpe2fs. mke2fs reserves enough for 1024x the creation
# size by default. From 64 MB that is 64 GiB, so any card up to 64 GB is
# covered. The built image has 7 reserved blocks; with the one in use that
# is 8 descriptor blocks x 64 groups per block x 128 MiB per group = 64 GiB.
#
# `-E resize=<blocks>` is mke2fs's option to reserve for more. It does
# nothing here: for reproducible builds wic appends its own
# `-E hash_seed=...` to every ext4 it makes (poky/scripts/lib/wic/
# partition.py, get_hash_seed_ext4), and mke2fs keeps only the LAST -E.
# Found 2026-11-07: the image came out with 7 reserved blocks, not the 31
# asked for, and the host reproduces it exactly (-E resize=... alone: 31;
# followed by a second -E: 7). So it is not in the options below.
#
# Past 64 GiB the kernel's ext4 driver can still grow a mounted filesystem
# by switching to a different descriptor layout (meta_bg), but that is
# untested here. To raise the guaranteed limit instead, make the partition
# bigger: --size 128 gives 128 GiB, for 64 MB more written per flash.

part /boot --source bootimg-partition --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype=vfat --label boot --active --align 4096 --size 100
part / --source rootfs --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype=ext4 --label root --align 4096
part --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype=ext4 --label captures --align 4096 --size 64 --mkfs-extraopts "-b 4096 -i 262144 -m 0"
